{"agent":"fleet-fixes-n","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"The refactor replaces the fragment `for another {-N}s` with whatever `left_text` returns, but `left_text` only honours the `unit=\"another\"` contract when `remaining_s >= 0`; on the negative path it ignores `unit` and returns a complete clause (\"ran out Ns ago, in its grace period\"). The caller's template `f\"leased by {held.holder} {held.left_text(..., unit='another')}\"` therefore produces, for exactly the scenario the bug report describes (lapse a lease past `ttl_s` but inside `grace_s`, then run `ddflow next`): `leased by a ran out 6s ago, in its grace period` instead of something like `leased by a for another 6s (grace period)`. The message that used to read `leased by a for another -6s` is still not well formed after the fix — the countdown is no longer negative, but the sentence is now ungrammatical and self-contradictory for the blocked agent (\"leased by a ran out…\" reads as if the lease were released, while `item_blocker` is in fact returning a conflict). The same helper in `reporting.py` is fine because that call site never passes `unit=\"another\"`, so the defect is only visible from `ddflow next`/plan's conflict detail; the in-process assertion in the test (`assert \"another -\" not in why and \"grace period\" in why`) passes because it checks substrings only and never asserts the shape of the produced sentence, so nothing in the suite would catch it.","digest":"c919d1c78a5d9808","location":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:487 (`item_blocker` lease-conflict message via `Lease.left_text`)","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:487 (`item_blocker` lease-conflict message via `Lease.left_text`)"}],"chunks_total":1,"combined_gates":["rubber_duck","critic"],"coverage":"1/1 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":4483,"diff_sha":"cf6e5895c58f4a9fb7ffce2f22cc5496ddb783aab6443809044f51a4d8cda8be","diff_source":"main..HEAD + 